.SH "DESCRIPTION"
\fBhdw4s\fR gives an existing account on an existing machine a full GNOME desktop that is reachable from a web browser\. It needs no graphics card and no container: the point is to reach the machine and the account that are already there\.
.P
Each session is a systemd unit that runs, as that user, an X server on the \fBdummy\fR driver, a GNOME session, and the Selkies streaming server\. The X server never touches a virtual terminal, a DRM device or an input device, which is what lets an ordinary account start one from a service with no seat and no login\.
.P
Sessions are expected to sit behind a reverse proxy that terminates TLS and identifies the user\. The streaming server itself runs with authentication switched off, so that a user who is already known to the proxy lands on their desktop without a second login\. See \fBREVERSE PROXY AND SECURITY\fR\.
.SH "COMMANDS"
.TP
\fBlist\fR [\fB\-\-seats\fR]
Print every person's desktop with its user, port, whether it has a credential of its own, whether it keeps its settings apart from the home directory, its transport, its unit state and its current display\. The ephemeral pool is one line under the table: how many seats it has and how many are in use, with a warning once 80% of them are, while there is still time to add seats before a visitor is turned away\.
.IP
With \fB\-\-seats\fR, list the pool's seats and the template\-authoring slot instead: each one's unit state, its display, and what is holding it \-\- for finding out what is wrong with the pool, not for configuring it\.
.TP
\fBcheck\fR
Report any running session that is not publishing the identity a returning browser tab compares itself against, whether the machine can hand anybody a desktop at all, and whether the memory limit on the desktop slice is a number this machine can actually reach \-\- and, with \fB/shared\fR on, whether desktops are being given a table and anybody is sweeping it\. Exit non\-zero if any of those is wrong\. Nothing is restarted: the point is that somebody is told which session and why\. See DIAGNOSTICS\.
.TP
\fBshow\fR \fIinstance\fR
Print one session's settings and, for each of them, which file it came from\.
.TP
\fBenable\fR \fIinstance\fR
Allocate a slot for a session and open its front door\. The desktop is not started here and is not started at boot: the socket listens, and the first connection to arrive starts the session behind it\. Safe to re\-run\. Refused for a seat of the ephemeral pool and for the template\-authoring slot: neither is a person's desktop, and the refusal says what to run instead\.
.TP
\fBdisable\fR \fIinstance\fR
Stop a session and close its front door, so nothing starts it again\. The slot stays reserved, so the port does not change if it is enabled later\. Refused for a seat of the ephemeral pool, where it would make the pool smaller with nothing reporting it, and for the template\-authoring slot\.
.TP
\fBnoauth\fR \fIinstance\fR
Remove the credential \fBauth\fR added, and explain when running without one is a reasonable choice\. Editing the file by hand is the same decision made quietly; this is the supported way to make it\.
.TP
\fBreap\fR
Stop sessions nobody has connected to for \fBHDW4S_IDLE_DAYS\fR\. Run from a timer; there is no need to invoke it by hand\. Stopping a named session keeps its files and settings; stopping an ephemeral slot destroys the session, because its home and profile are held in memory\.
.TP
\fBrelease\fR [\fB\-\-internal\fR] \fIinstance\fR
Stop a session and give up its slot, so another session may take the port\. The session's profile directory is left in place\.
.IP
A seat of the ephemeral pool is never released by name: \fBpool size\fR chooses which seats go\. The template\-authoring slot is released only with \fB\-\-internal\fR, which is refused for anything else\. Releasing it is never needed \-\- \fBtemplate reset\fR empties the template \-\- but is harmless: the next \fBtemplate edit\fR sets up a fresh one\.
.TP
\fBtransport\fR \fIinstance\fR \fBtcp\fR|\fBunix\fR
Choose how the reverse proxy reaches this session\. See \fBTRANSPORTS\fR\. An ephemeral slot is not offered the choice: it is always reached over its Unix socket, and \fBtcp\fR is refused for one\.
.TP
\fBauth\fR \fIinstance\fR
Require the proxy to present a secret as well as an acceptable address\. Generated here, sealed to this machine, and never shown to the user, so there is still no login screen\.
.IP
Refused for an ephemeral slot, where a credential excludes nobody: the socket is group\-owned by the reverse proxy and open to nothing else, so the only caller that can reach it is the one that would be given the secret\. What it would do instead is answer that proxy with 401 until the proxy is configured with it, so one session refuses connections its neighbours accept for a reason no listing explains\.
.TP
\fBproxy\fR \fIinstance\fR
Print an nginx configuration for this session, matching whichever transport and authentication it is set up for\. Refused for a seat of the ephemeral pool and for the template\-authoring slot, which are reached through the pool's one hostname: \fBpool proxy\fR prints that\.
.TP
\fBpool size\fR [\fIN\fR]
With no number, print how many seats the ephemeral pool has, which of them are in use and why, and anything about the pool that does not agree with \fBHDW4S_EPHEMERAL_SLOTS\fR\. With a number, make the pool that size \fBnow\fR, with no reboot: new seats are set up and offered to visitors at once, and seats are taken away from the highest\-numbered down\. See \fBTHE EPHEMERAL POOL\fR\.
.IP
A seat a visitor is using is never taken away, whether or not anybody is looking at it at the moment\. The pool then stops one above it, the command says which seat held it there and how to end that desktop, and it exits non\-zero\. Running it again later with the same number finishes the job, and so does running it again after it was interrupted\.
.TP
\fBpool show\fR
What \fBpool size\fR prints, then every setting an ephemeral desktop starts with, its value, and the file it came from\. Also lists any seat that still has a setting of its own \-\- left by configuring seats one by one before \fBpool set\fR existed \-\- since that seat's visitors get something the others do not\.
.TP
\fBpool set\fR \fIKEY\fR=\fIVALUE\fR\|\.\|\.\|\.
Change a setting for every ephemeral desktop, and for the template\-authoring desktop, so that a template is made on the desktop visitors get\. Written to \fB/etc/hdw4s/_hdw4s_pool\.conf\fR, which those desktops read after \fBhdw4s\.conf\fR and which wins over it; named desktops do not read it\. It applies to each desktop that starts afterwards: one a visitor has open keeps what it started with until it ends, and nothing tells you to restart it\.
.IP
The settings read once for the whole machine rather than by each desktop \-\- the \fBHDW4S_EPHEMERAL_*\fR sizes, background and URL \-\- are written to \fBhdw4s\.conf\fR, where they are read, and the command says when they apply\. Refused: settings a pool desktop has a fixed answer to (\fBHDW4S_ISOLATION\fR, \fBHDW4S_PROFILE_DIR\fR, \fBHDW4S_TRANSPORT\fR, \fBHDW4S_AUTH\fR, \fBHDW4S_PROXY_GROUP\fR), settings that apply to the whole machine (use \fBset\fR), \fBHDW4S_EPHEMERAL_SLOTS\fR (use \fBpool size\fR), and an \fBHDW4S_IDLE_DAYS\fR of \fB0\fR, which would never free a seat\.
.TP
\fBpool unset\fR \fIKEY\fR
Comment a pool setting out, so the machine's value applies to the pool again\. Refused for \fBHDW4S_IDLE_DAYS\fR while the machine's value is \fB0\fR, since the pool would then never be reaped\.
.TP
\fBpool proxy\fR
Print the nginx configuration for the pool: one hostname, pointed at the pool's front door\. See \fBTHE EPHEMERAL POOL\fR\.
.TP
\fBset\fR [\fIinstance\fR] \fIKEY\fR=\fIVALUE\fR\|\.\|\.\|\.
Change a setting without opening an editor\. With an instance, writes to that session's file; without one, to the defaults\. The edit is deliberately conservative: an existing assignment is changed where it stands, keeping its indentation and any note written after it on the same line; otherwise the documented default is uncommented in place, so the explanation above it still applies; otherwise the setting is appended\. Nothing else in the file is touched, and anything not offered here can still be edited by hand\. \fBHDW4S_EPHEMERAL_SLOTS\fR is refused, here and by \fBunset\fR: it is written by \fBpool size\fR, which changes the seats along with it\. A seat of the ephemeral pool and the template\-authoring slot are refused as an instance, here and by \fBunset\fR: a visitor is handed whichever seat is free, so the pool's desktops are configured together, with \fBpool set\fR\.
.TP
\fBunset\fR [\fIinstance\fR] \fIKEY\fR
Comment a setting out so the default applies again\. The line is commented rather than deleted, so the value that was there stays visible\.
.TP
\fBseed\fR \fIinstance\fR [\fB\-\-only\fR \fIpath\fR]\|\.\|\.\|\.
Copy an account's existing desktop settings from its home directory into an isolated session's profile, once\. Only ever in that direction, only when asked, and only into a profile that is still empty \-\- a profile with anything in it is one somebody has used, and there is no way to tell which of two copies of a setting is the newer\. Re\-running is refused rather than overwriting; remove the profile first if starting over is really meant\.
.IP
This is a snapshot and not a link\. The two diverge from that moment on\.
.IP
\fB\-\-only\fR limits the copy to the named paths, relative to the home directory, and may be given more than once\. An account that has been in use for years accumulates settings for programs that will never run in this session; copying all of it forward only carries the accumulation into a second place\. Naming what matters \-\- the settings database, the browser profile, the handful of applications actually used \-\- is usually a great deal less than what is there\. Paths that do not exist are reported and skipped, and the exclusions above still apply inside the paths that do\.
.IP "" 4
.nf
hdw4s seed alice \-\-only \.config/dconf \-\-only \.config/google\-chrome
.fi
.IP "" 0
.IP
Caches are skipped, including the ones browsers keep inside their profile directories rather than under \fB~/\.cache\fR; they are regenerable and are usually almost all of the size\. So are downloaded models and component data: a current Chrome fetches an on\-device language model of nearly three gigabytes, per profile, which dwarfs everything worth carrying across\. Saved passwords are skipped too, since the session has its own keyring with its own password\.
.IP
The session must be stopped, or the copy captures half\-written state\.
.TP
\fBkeyring\fR \fIinstance\fR
Give a session its own keyring with a generated password, sealed to this machine with systemd\-creds and handed to the session at start time, so it is unlocked without anyone typing anything\. The session's keyring is separate from the account's login keyring: passwords saved in one are not visible in the other\. Requires root and a restart of the session\.
.TP
\fBtemplate edit\fR [\fB\-\-yes\fR]
Change what every new ephemeral desktop starts from\. Starts the template\-authoring desktop \-\- setting it up the first time, so there is nothing to do beforehand \-\- and prints a link that works \fBonce, within five minutes\fR\. Open it, change what you like, and \fBlog out\fR of that desktop when done: the command then asks whether to publish the changes\. Interrupting the command stops the desktop and keeps nothing\. It is for editing, not for working in: nothing but this command starts that desktop, and one left running by a command that was killed is stopped, unpublished, by the next \fBtemplate edit\fR\.
.IP
What is published is an allow\-list, so a setting nobody thought of is left out rather than handed to every visitor\. Carried: GNOME settings, including everything GNOME Tweaks sets (the background, the dock, focus mode, desktop icons, fonts, window buttons, extensions' settings; not notifications, recent folders, command histories, or weather and world\-clock locations), pictures added as backgrounds, startup applications, default applications, GNOME Shell extensions, fonts, icons and themes installed for the user, and Chrome's extensions, bookmarks and preferences\. \fBNot carried\fR: Chrome's cookies, history, saved logins, autofill, open tabs, site storage, or any extension's own stored data \-\- extensions arrive installed, with their defaults\. Signing in to a Google account in the authoring desktop is refused by the managed Chrome policy; visitors' own desktops allow it\. Nothing else from the home directory is carried unless \fBtemplate keep\fR names it\. A setting that names a file only the authoring desktop had is left out and reported, and the publish lists every command the template makes a visitor's desktop run\.
.IP
Set \fBHDW4S_EPHEMERAL_URL\fR to have the full link printed\. Running desktops keep what they started with\.
.TP
\fBtemplate keep\fR [\fIpath\fR\|\.\|\.\|\.]
Also carry a program's own settings, which the built\-in list leaves out \-\- say the preconfigured printers of a slicer, or an IDE's board URLs\. Name the path as it is in a home directory:
.IP "" 4
.nf
hdw4s template keep ~/\.config/PrusaSlicer ~/\.config/OpenSCAD
hdw4s template keep ~/\.arduinoIDE
.fi
.IP "" 0
.IP
Where a program following XDG actually keeps it inside the desktop is worked out for you\. The next published \fBtemplate edit\fR carries it whole, minus anything in it named like browser storage, a history or a credential, and it reports, by name only, files that look as though they hold a password or token, and anything large: every new desktop copies the template into memory as it starts, so install big content (board cores, libraries) system\-wide rather than keeping it\. Refused outright: Chrome's profile, keys and keyrings, caches, and anything as broad as \fB~/\.config\fR\. The program itself must be installed on the machine\. With no path, lists what is kept\.
.TP
\fBtemplate forget\fR \fIpath\fR\|\.\|\.\|\.
Stop carrying a kept path, from the next publish on\.
.TP
\fBtemplate reset\fR
Remove the template: new ephemeral desktops start from the stock desktop\. The way back when a published template has broken something\.
.TP
\fBtemplate show\fR
List the settings and files the template carries\.
.TP
\fBtemplate copyfrom\fR [\fIuser\fR\fB@\fR]\fIhost\fR | \fIfile\fR
Publish another hdw4s machine's template here, as an edit here would be, including this machine's settings database\. Given a host, one \fBrsync\fR pulls what that machine has published; the command is printed before it runs, so a failure can be retried or debugged by hand, and reaching the other machine as root is left to the administrator\. Given a file, it is an archive \fBtemplate export\fR wrote: the route for machines that cannot reach each other\. The archive is unpacked refusing absolute paths, \fB\.\.\fR, device files and links that lead outside it\.
.TP
\fBtemplate export\fR \fIfile\fR
Write the template published here to one archive, for \fBtemplate copyfrom\fR on another machine\. An existing file is never overwritten\.
.TP
\fBfirewall \-\-apply\fR
Rewrite the package's nftables table and the kernel's reserved\-port list from the current configuration\. Run this after changing \fBHDW4S_PROXIES\fR, \fBHDW4S_BASE_PORT\fR or \fBHDW4S_BLOCK_SIZE\fR\.
.TP
\fBfirewall \-\-check\fR
Report whether the table is loaded and whether every session listens on a port the table actually covers\. Exits non\-zero if not\.
.TP
\fBfirewall \-\-restore\fR
Put the table back if it has gone missing, and do nothing if it has not\. Run periodically by \fBhdw4s\-firewall\.timer\fR; see \fBREVERSE PROXY AND SECURITY\fR\.
.TP
\fBfirewall \-\-print\fR
Write the ruleset to standard output without loading it\.
.SH "INSTANCES"
An instance is a user name\. An account has one desktop:
.IP "" 4
.nf
hdw4s enable alice
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Versions up to 1\.1 also accepted \fBalice:2\fR, a second concurrent desktop for the same account on the same machine\. That form is no longer created\.
.P
It was withdrawn because it was not worth what it cost to reason about, not because it stopped working\. A second desktop doubled the states every part of the package had to account for \-\- two instances against one home directory, one keyring, one set of application single\-instance locks \-\- to deliver something that can be had instead by giving the account a desktop on a second machine, which is a case this package already supports properly\.
.P
\fBAn instance created by an earlier version keeps working\fR, and keeps its slot and its port\. \fBhdw4s list\fR reports it with the state \fBlegacy\fR\. No command will accept the name except \fBhdw4s release\fR, which stops the session first, so retiring one takes a single command:
.IP "" 4
.nf
hdw4s release alice:2
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
An account wanting desktops on \fIseveral machines\fR at once is a different thing and is supported; see \fBHDW4S_ISOLATION\fR\.
.P
\fBLogging out ends the desktop\.\fR Logging out from the desktop's own menu stops its session cleanly; nothing starts it again until somebody visits its address, and that visit starts a fresh desktop with no login screen\. An open browser tab that reconnects counts as a visit\. A desktop nobody is connected to costs nothing, so this is also how to restart one from inside it\.
.P
\fBA desktop that keeps failing is not started again\.\fR Each run that ends in failure \-\- not a logout, not a stop \-\- is recorded in \fB/run/hdw4s/ledger/<instance>\fR\. After three inside twenty minutes the next start refuses before the desktop comes up, and a visitor gets a page naming the desktop, when it last failed, when it will be tried again by itself, and the commands below\. \fBhdw4s check\fR reports it, and the journal says so once at warning priority\. Find the cause in \fBjournalctl \-u hdw4s@<instance>\fR, then:
.IP "" 4
.nf
systemctl reset\-failed hdw4s@<instance>\.service
rm \-f /run/hdw4s/ledger/<instance>
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
The unit's own start limit stays behind this as a fuse, for a failure that happens before the session's check can run; it is sized so that ordinary use, logouts included, does not reach it, and it also lifts by itself, within an hour\. A visit to a desktop that is refusing starts no desktop and is not counted as a failure, so it does not keep the refusal alive\.
.SH "CONFIGURATION"
Two files are read in order, the second overriding the first:
.IP "" 4
.nf
/etc/hdw4s/hdw4s\.conf         defaults for every session
/etc/hdw4s/<instance>\.conf    settings for one session
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
\fBhdw4s show\fR \fIinstance\fR prints the settings that apply to one session\.
.P
Most settings work per session for free, because a session reads the shared file and then its own\. Six do not, and \fBhdw4s set\fR refuses to write them to a session's file rather than accepting something that would have no effect: \fBHDW4S_PROXIES\fR, \fBHDW4S_BASE_PORT\fR, \fBHDW4S_BLOCK_SIZE\fR and \fBHDW4S_MEDIA_PORTS\fR are read when the firewall is applied, which happens once for the machine; \fBHDW4S_ALLOW_SYSTEM_USER\fR is read before a session exists; and \fBSELKIES_VERSION\fR belongs to the copy of the streaming server, of which there is one\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_PROXIES\fR: Addresses allowed to reach a session, separated by spaces; IPv4 and IPv6 addresses and prefixes are both accepted\. Empty means this machine only\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_ISOLATION\fR: \fBnone\fR or \fBprofile\fR\. See \fBSHARED HOME DIRECTORIES\fR\. Defaults to \fBnone\fR\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_PROFILE_DIR\fR: Where an isolated session keeps its private profile\. Defaults to \fB/var/lib/hdw4s\fR\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_BASE_PORT\fR, \fBHDW4S_BLOCK_SIZE\fR: The block of TCP ports sessions are allocated from\. Defaults to 7300 and
.IP "1." 4
Two consecutive blocks are actually reserved: the first is what the reverse proxy connects to, and the second, immediately above it, is kept out of the kernel's ephemeral range and closed to connections from any desktop\. Nothing listens in the second block: each session's streaming server listens on a filesystem socket instead (see FILES)\. So the defaults reserve 7300\-7427, and only 7300\-7363 are ever reachable from off the machine\.
.IP "" 0

.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_ALLOW_SYSTEM_USER\fR: Set to \fByes\fR to permit a session for an account below UID 1000\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_TRANSPORT\fR: \fBtcp\fR or \fBunix\fR\. Set with \fBhdw4s transport\fR, which also arranges the units that go with it, rather than by hand\. It applies to named desktops only: an ephemeral slot is always \fBunix\fR, whatever this says, because nothing outside the machine addresses one\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_PROXY_GROUP\fR: The group allowed to open a session's Unix socket, i\.e\. the group the reverse proxy runs as\. Defaults to \fBwww\-data\fR\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_AUTH\fR: \fBnone\fR or \fBbasic\fR\. \fBPer\-session only\fR: this is state the tool writes into a session's own file, and putting it in the shared configuration does nothing\. Turn it on with \fBhdw4s auth\fR, which also generates the secret, and off with \fBhdw4s noauth\fR\.
.IP
\fBhdw4s enable\fR turns it on for a session it creates, because the firewall filters what arrives from other machines and nothing stands between the session and the other accounts on this one: the streaming server listens on loopback, and any local account can open a loopback port\. The reverse proxy presents the secret and the user never sees it, so there is no login screen either way\. Re\-running \fBhdw4s enable\fR on a session that already exists leaves the setting alone\.
.IP
There is deliberately no site\-wide default\. One existed and was removed: a shared switch for a security\-relevant setting is chosen once, inherited by every session created afterwards, and invisible at the moment it takes effect \-\- so the session that ends up without a credential is not the one anybody decided about\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_SESSION\fR: The desktop to start; defaults to \fBgnome\-session\fR\. Anything that runs on X11 works, and nothing else in a session depends on the choice\. GNOME 50, which Ubuntu 26\.04 ships, removes the X11 session that the current Selkies release needs, whereas Xorg and the lighter desktops have no such plan, so changing this is the whole migration when that matters\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_IDLE_DAYS\fR: How long a session may go with nobody connected before \fBreap\fR stops it\. Defaults to 7\. Measured by connections rather than by typing, so work left running is not mistaken for an idle desktop\. Connections are looked for every five minutes rather than once a day, because a desktop used only during working hours has nobody connected to it at any moment a nightly check would happen to look\.
.IP
A bare number means days, and a unit letter overrides that: \fBs\fR seconds, \fBm\fR minutes, \fBh\fR hours, \fBd\fR days, \fBw\fR weeks, \fBM\fR months, \fBy\fR years, as systemd spells them\. Case matters, because \fBm\fR is minutes and \fBM\fR is months\. Fractions are allowed, so \fB0\.5h\fR is thirty minutes\. One unit at a time: write \fB90m\fR rather than \fB1h30m\fR\.
.IP
A value this cannot read is an error, and nothing falls back to a default\. A session whose setting cannot be read is left running and \fBreap\fR says so every time it looks, rather than being stopped on a number nobody wrote\.
.IP
The shortest window is 120 seconds\. An ephemeral session takes up to 91 seconds to shut down, because several of the desktop's helpers have to be killed rather than asked, so a window shorter than that cannot free a slot inside itself\. Connections are sampled every five minutes in any case, so a window near the floor is noticed at the next sample rather than on time\.
.IP
Set it to 0 to never reap\. That is a choice for a named session, where stopping one is a nap: its files and settings stay and the next connection starts it again\.
.IP
\fBIt is refused for an ephemeral slot\.\fR The same clock governs one, but stopping it is not reversible \(em the home and the profile are held in memory and go with the session \(em and reaping is the only thing that ever frees the slot, because a visitor who closes the tab tells nothing\. With no window, unattended desktops accumulate until the pool is full and every visitor after that is refused, from one configuration line and with nothing reporting why\. To let the pool's desktops sit longer without moving every session, give the pool a longer window of its own:
.IP "" 4
.nf
hdw4s pool set HDW4S_IDLE_DAYS=30d
.fi
.IP "" 0
.IP
Setting 0 without an instance name is refused too, on a machine that has ephemeral slots, since that value reaches them as well \-\- unless the pool has a window of its own, which it reads instead\. Set 0 on the named sessions that should never be stopped, or give the pool its own window first\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_MEDIA_PORTS\fR: Either \fBproxied\fR, the default, or \fBdirect\fR\. The streaming server scatters connection candidates across the ephemeral port range on every address the machine has\. \fBproxied\fR drops new inbound connections to that range, which closes them; \fBdirect\fR leaves them reachable, which is needed only when a browser reaches the streaming server without a proxy in between\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_RESIZE\fR: Whether the desktop follows the size of the browser window\. Defaults to true\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_LANG\fR: The locale the session runs in\. Unset, the machine's own \fBLANG\fR from \fB/etc/default/locale\fR is used, and \fBC\.UTF\-8\fR only if that is absent\. It has to be a UTF\-8 locale: some terminals refuse to start otherwise\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_FRAMERATE\fR: Where a session starts, and how far a client may move it from the video settings panel\. Defaults to \fB30,8\-60\fR: it starts at 30, and someone who wants smoother motion can ask for up to 60\. A bare number is a hard ceiling instead, so \fB30\fR means 30 and nothing else \-\- which is what an administrator who pinned a rate gets to keep\.
.IP
Measured on one session at 1280x656, over 25 seconds, both reaching the rate they were given: with a GPU, 30fps cost 16% of a core and 60fps 19%; without one, 34% and 49%\. So the upper half of the default range is nearly free on a machine that can encode in hardware, and costs about half a core per session that asks for it on one that cannot\. 120 measured at 27% on hardware\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_ENCODER\fR: Passed through to the streaming server\. Leave it alone unless the picture is visibly wrong\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_DPI\fR: Whether the desktop's display density follows the browser's, which is what makes text the right size on a HiDPI screen or in a zoomed window\. Defaults to \fBfollow\fR\. Set a number to pin it, which makes the server refuse the density a client asks for and so stops it rewriting \fB~/\.Xresources\fR and \fB~/\.xsettingsd\fR again and again; see SHARED HOME DIRECTORIES\. A pinned number other than 96 is still applied once when the session starts, so both files are written that once\. Pinning 96 writes neither, ever\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_SCREEN_LOCK\fR: Whether a named desktop's user may lock its screen: \fBoff\fR (the default) or \fBon\fR\. Off, locking is disabled and locked down \-\- Super+L, the menu entry and idle locking all do nothing \-\- because locking a remote screen protects little (the browser tab is the access control) and is one slip of a ChromeOS launcher key away\. The machine's own dconf profile is kept: this adds one database beneath it rather than replacing it\. Ephemeral desktops never lock: nobody could unlock them\. Takes effect at the next restart\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_INDEXING\fR: Whether the desktop's file indexer runs\. Defaults to \fBoff\fR, and takes effect only with \fBHDW4S_ISOLATION=profile\fR; without that the indexer is the account's rather than the session's and is left alone, with a warning\. See SHARED HOME DIRECTORIES for why, and for what turning it off costs\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_THUMBNAILS\fR: Whether the file manager draws image thumbnails\. Defaults to \fBoff\fR, and like \fBHDW4S_INDEXING\fR takes effect only with \fBHDW4S_ISOLATION=profile\fR\. The file manager's own \fBlocal\-only\fR setting does not protect a network home, because the kernel calls NFS native; see SHARED HOME DIRECTORIES\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_STARTUP_HOLD\fR: Whether the streaming server waits for the desktop to finish starting before it starts\. Defaults to \fBon\fR\. GNOME 46 can leave a starting desktop black for good in two ways: input that arrives just before its desktop is first shown, and a screen size change in the seconds after that, before it has finished starting\. A browser that connects early sends both\. The wait ends when GNOME Shell signals the end of its startup, which costs the visitor about three seconds they spend waiting for the desktop to paint anyway; a shell that starts into the overview or with a dialog open gives no such signal, and is released eighteen seconds after its desktop is shown\. If the desktop is never shown, the wait gives up before the session's start timeout and starts the stream regardless, and on a desktop without GNOME's compositor it does not wait at all\. Each session logs how long it waited and why it stopped\. It does not cover GNOME Shell being restarted while a visitor is connected\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_HIDPI\fR: How a high\-density client is handled: \fBscale\fR (the default) asks for the size of the browser window and lets the browser stretch the result, which is correct size and slightly soft; \fBnative\fR asks for the client's full device resolution, which is pixel\-exact and, on GNOME, half size\. See SHARED HOME DIRECTORIES for why the desktop does not scale itself\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_MICROPHONE\fR: Whether these desktops may be heard\. Defaults to \fBno\fR, which passes \fBmicrophone\-enabled=false|locked\fR to the streaming server \-\- the one condition on which it refuses microphone data outright, so a desktop cannot be listened to by a client that asks nicely or through a flaw in the page\. Set it to \fByes\fR and the refusal is lifted while the lock stays on, so the operator still decides and a connected page still cannot change it\. It does not switch anyone's microphone on, and deliberately does not start the uplink either: a session that asks the browser for a device as it connects never starts its video stream at all when that request cannot be satisfied \-\- a declined prompt, a machine with no microphone \-\- and the desktop stays blank with nothing said\. So the capture device inside the session appears once the uplink is turned on from the session's own menu, and not before\. Read when a session starts, so a restart applies it\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_WEBCAM\fR: Whether these desktops may see a camera\. Defaults to \fBno\fR, which passes \fBwebcam\-enabled=false|locked\fR \-\- the one condition on which the streaming server turns webcam frames away\. \fByes\fR lifts the refusal, keeps the lock, and additionally preloads the V4L2 interposer into the session's applications\. That last part is not optional: a container has no camera and no kernel device for one, so the interposer is what answers a program's camera calls, out of a socket the streaming server serves\. It is preloaded into the session and deliberately \fBnot\fR into the streaming server, which is the other end of that socket\. Read when a session starts\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBSELKIES_VERSION\fR: Pin a Selkies release and stop following upstream\.
.IP "" 0
.SH "RUNNING SOMETHING AS ROOT"
\fBsudo\fR works the way it does everywhere else\. \fBsudoers\fR and PAM decide who may use it, a password is asked for and a wrong one is refused, and this package adds no rule, wrapper or exception of its own\.
.P
It did not always, and the reason is worth keeping because it looked deliberate\. Until 1\.2 the session unit's capability bounding set held \fBCAP_NET_RAW\fR and nothing else\. A setuid\-root program without \fBCAP_SETGID\fR cannot change group, so \fBsudo\fR failed at \fBunable to change to root gid\fR \-\- before \fBsudoers\fR was consulted, before anyone was asked for a password, and with nothing said about why\. An administrator's own desktop refused them root while \fBsudoers\fR said they could have it\.
.P
That read as a security control and was not one\. A setuid\-root program reaches uid 0 whatever the bounding set says, because the kernel grants the uid at \fBexecve\fR; and a process with uid 0 owns every root\-owned file through the ordinary permission bits, needing no capability to do it\. Run under exactly that bounding set, a setuid\-root test binary reported:
.IP "" 4
.nf
euid=0  caps=cap_net_raw=ep
/etc/shadow                       r : OK
/etc/sudoers                      r : OK
/root/canary                      w : OK
/etc/systemd/system/probe\.service w : OK
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
The missing capability stopped the \fIgroup\fR change, which only programs on the legitimate path \-\- \fBsudo\fR, \fBsu\fR, \fBlogin\fR \-\- ever ask for\. It could not deny root to anybody \fBsudoers\fR denies it to\. It could only deny it to the administrators \fBsudoers\fR allows\.
.P
What does constrain a root process inside a session is the unit's sandbox, not its capabilities\. \fBProtectSystem=strict\fR makes the file system read\-only apart from the session's own directories, so the writes above fail there \-\- with one exception: it leaves \fB/run\fR writable, and the machine's \fB/run\fR is what a session sees there, so a root process in a session can write in it\. Devices are hidden and kernel tunables are not writable\. \fBCAP_SYS_ADMIN\fR, \fBCAP_SYS_MODULE\fR and \fBCAP_NET_ADMIN\fR are still out of the bounding set, so mounting, loading a module and reconfiguring the network stay impossible for uid 0 in a session\.
.IP "" 4
.nf
sudo systemd\-run \-\-pty \-\-collect \-\-uid=0 \-\- bash
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
is the way out to a root shell without those restrictions\. systemd does not consult polkit for a caller that is already root, and the shell it starts is a transient unit outside the session's control group \-\- which also means it is named in the journal, rather than being an unremarked setuid transition\.
.P
\fBA desktop whose account can \fBsudo\fR is a desktop whose compromise is root\.\fR That is true of any Linux machine\. What is particular here is that the desktop is reachable through a reverse proxy, so more people are in a position to try\. If an account should not administer the machine from a browser, say so in \fBsudoers\fR: that is where an administrator looks for the answer, and it is the only place that decides it\.
.SH "SHARED HOME DIRECTORIES"
A GNOME session assumes it is the only one using its home directory\. That assumption breaks when the same home is mounted on more than one machine and a desktop runs against it on each \-\- the case this section exists for, and the one way an account gets more than one desktop\. Those sessions then write the same settings database, the same keyring and the same metadata stores\. None of those are safe to share, and the settings database and the metadata stores use memory\-mapped and journalled files that are documented not to work over NFS at all\.
.P
Setting \fBHDW4S_ISOLATION=profile\fR keeps the shared home for the user's files but moves most of the session's settings, state and cache to a private directory on local disk \-\- most, because the streaming server writes three paths it builds from the home directory itself, which no XDG variable reaches\. Concretely, the session gets its own \fBXDG_DATA_HOME\fR, \fBXDG_STATE_HOME\fR, \fBXDG_CACHE_HOME\fR and dconf profile, its own X authority cookie, its own audio server socket, and wrappers that give Firefox and Thunderbird their own profiles\.
.P
All four XDG base directories move together: config, data, state and cache\. Leaving the config home shared and redirecting dconf on its own was tried and abandoned, because it needs a service that has to answer before any setting can be read, and it leaves a lock beside its database that a killed session does not clean up\. What this does not cover is a path a program builds from the home directory rather than from these variables\. Some input\-method configuration hardcodes \fB~/\.config\fR that way, and so does the streaming server\.
.P
What stays shared:
.IP "" 4
.nf
Documents, Desktop, Downloads, Pictures, Music, Videos
everything else in the home directory, including ~/\.ssh
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Which of those folders a home has, and where they are, belongs to the home: \fB~/\.config/user\-dirs\.dirs\fR and \fB~/\.config/user\-dirs\.locale\fR say it\. A named desktop with its own profile reads and writes a copy of both in the profile, as every program in it expects, and keeps that copy in step with the home's in both directions while it runs\. So a folder moved inside the desktop the supported way \-\- \fBxdg\-user\-dirs\-update \-\-set\fR, or an answer to the "Update standard folders" dialog \-\- reaches the home within seconds, and from there every desktop on the same home, including one already running on another machine\. Which side changed is decided by content, never by the clock; when both did, the home's copy wins and the journal says so\. Those two files are all this writes in the home: it never creates \fB~/\.config\fR, and it never replaces either file where it is a symbolic link\. While the home has a \fBuser\-dirs\.dirs\fR, the profile's \fBuser\-dirs\.conf\fR switches off the login run of \fBxdg\-user\-dirs\-update\fR, so that no machine recreates default folders in a home whose owner moved or hid them; a home without one still gets the usual first run, and its result is carried home\.
.P
That last line covers files the software writes as well as the user's own, and three of them come from the streaming server rather than from the desktop:
.IP "" 4
.nf
~/\.Xresources   written when a client reports its display density\.
                The user's own lines survive; their Xft\.dpi is replaced\.
~/\.xsettingsd   written on the same trigger, and rewritten whole, so
                anything already in it is lost\. Nothing on a GNOME
                system reads it, and the daemon that would is not part
                of this package\.
~/Desktop       would be created at startup as the destination for file
                transfers, which this package does not enable\. Pointed
                at the session's runtime directory instead, so it is
                not created here at all\.
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
A high\-density client is a related trap\. The desktop does not scale itself: GNOME works its scale out from the monitor's physical size, a virtual monitor reports none, and GNOME therefore stops at 1\. Asking the streaming server for a higher DPI does not help either, because on GNOME that reaches only the X resource database, which the settings daemon owns\. So a client on a 2x screen asking for its full device resolution gets a desktop at half size\. \fBHDW4S_HIDPI\fR defaults to \fBscale\fR for that reason\.
.P
The indexer is a separate matter, and is off by default \-\- see \fBHDW4S_INDEXING\fR\. On a home shared between machines it is work without an answer: every machine crawls the same tree over the network, each keeps its own copy of the result on local disk, and none of them sees a change made from another machine, because the only change notification it has is inotify and inotify does not cross NFS\. It is not removed and not masked: the package is a hard dependency of the file manager, and masking it would reach the account's real login session on the same machine\. The session sets the keys that Settings \-> Search \-> Search Locations writes, so a user can turn it back on for their own session where they would think to look \-\- until the desktop next starts, because while \fBHDW4S_INDEXING\fR is \fBoff\fR they are written again at every start; the setting is the lasting way\. The cost is that searching inside documents stops working; searching by name still works, but walks the tree instead of consulting an index\.
.P
Thumbnails go the same way and on weaker grounds, which is worth stating\. The file manager's own setting is \fBlocal\-only\fR, which sounds like the protection a network home wants and is not: \fBlocal\fR there means the kernel calls the filesystem native, and it calls NFS native\. So the default reads every image in a browsed directory in full, over the network\. Against that: the result is cached in the profile and the profile persists, so it is a cost per machine rather than per session; the file manager skips anything over its own size limit; and this is something a user can see and probably wants\. What tips it is that a desktop delivered as a video stream pays for a wall of thumbnails twice, once over the network and again in the encoder\. \fBHDW4S_THUMBNAILS=on\fR gives them back; while it is off, the file manager's own preference is set again at every start\.
.P
The first two are what \fBHDW4S_DPI\fR set to a number stops \-\- \fB96\fR stops them outright, another number still applies itself once at startup and writes them that once \-\- at the cost of a desktop that no longer scales to a HiDPI or zoomed browser\. It is left following by default because that cost falls on everyone who opens a session, while these two files matter only to an account that keeps something in them\.
.P
Existing settings can be carried across once with \fBhdw4s seed\fR, which is the only supported way to populate a profile from a home directory and does not run by itself\.
.P
What becomes per\-session:
.IP "" 4
.nf
desktop appearance, extensions, keyboard shortcuts
saved passwords and online accounts (a separate keyring; see `keyring`)
browser profile, history and open tabs
recently\-used files
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
One consequence is worth planning for: browsers download some data per profile, and an isolated session is a new profile\. A current Chrome fetches an on\-device language model of nearly three gigabytes that way, so a machine running several isolated sessions holds several copies of it on local disk and fetches each one over the network\. Where that matters, it can be turned off for every session at once with a browser policy, which is a decision for the administrator rather than something this package should make\.
.P
That split is not a compromise that can be tuned away\. Those components conflict precisely because they are shared, so the only way to stop the conflict is to stop sharing them\.
.SH "DISPLAY AND PORT ALLOCATION"
A session's TCP port comes from a slot recorded in \fB/etc/hdw4s/instances\fR\. The slot is assigned when the session is first enabled and never moves, so a reverse proxy and a firewall can both be configured against it and stay valid when accounts are added or removed\.
.P
The X display number is \fInot\fR allocated\. The X server picks the first free one itself and reports it back, which is the only way to claim one without a race\. Nothing outside the machine ever sees the display number, so it does not need to be predictable\.
.SH "THE EPHEMERAL POOL"
There are two kinds of session to configure: \fBnamed sessions\fR, one per person, each with its own hostname; and \fBthe ephemeral pool\fR, which grows as it is used and whose sessions nobody provisions\.
.P
The pool is configured as \fBone thing\fR, not slot by slot\. Visitors arrive at a single hostname for the whole pool and are given a session from it\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBOne hostname for the pool\.\fR Slots have no hostnames of their own\. Giving one its own name and pointing a proxy at it defeats the arrangement\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBThe pool chooses, and remembers\.\fR A returning visitor is given back the session they already have rather than a new desktop on every arrival, and where that cannot be decided safely the visitor is asked rather than acted on\. Reaching a slot directly skips that decision entirely\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBA slot must not be reached directly\.\fR Not by a reverse proxy, not by anything else\. The sockets under \fB/run/hdw4s/proxy/\fR belong to the pool\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBSeats have no port\.\fR They are reached over filesystem sockets, so they take nothing from the \fBHDW4S_BLOCK_SIZE\fR port block, which is for named desktops; their rows in the slot table are numbered from 1000\. Seats created by an earlier version keep the number they have\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHow many slots\.\fR A fresh install mints \fBone\fR and offers none until the pool is given a size; one is the number that is safe on a machine the package knows nothing about\. The ceiling is \fB99\fR, and it is the user\-id window rather than a policy: slots take consecutive ids from \fBHDW4S_EPHEMERAL_UID_BASE\fR inside 60900\-60999, and the top of that window is kept for the template\-authoring slot\. \fBpool size\fR refuses a number beyond it before changing anything\.
.IP
An idle slot costs an entry under \fB/run\fR and nothing else\. An occupied one costs about \fB560 MB\fR \-\- measured across sixteen concurrent attached desktops on this project's own image, flat from the first to the sixteenth \-\- so the number a machine can \fIserve\fR is its free memory divided by that, which is usually far smaller than the number of slots it is safe to \fImint\fR\. The per\-session sizes are ceilings rather than consumption: the same desktops used about 21 MB of a 3\.5 GB allowance\.
.IP
\fBThe slot count is the only admission control there is\.\fR Nothing asks whether the machine can afford a desktop before starting one, so a pool larger than the machine can serve is a machine that will run out of memory rather than one that turns somebody away\. Measured on a 16 GiB container with no swap and a pool of 32: served normally to 18, slower from 19, and at 28 the kernel's OOM killer began choosing victims inside desktops, which leaves a unit reporting \fBactive\fR behind a blank screen\. Size the pool for the machine\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBChanging the size\.\fR \fBhdw4s pool size N\fR, and nothing else\. Three things describe the pool \-\- \fBHDW4S_EPHEMERAL_SLOTS\fR, which the next boot mints identities from; the identities themselves, under \fB/run\fR; and one row per seat in the slot table, which is what visitors are offered \-\- and the command changes all three, in an order that an interruption cannot turn into a seat that is offered and cannot start\. Seats go from the top because the setting describes seats \fB0\fR to \fBN\-1\fR\. A seat a visitor is using holds the pool at one above it until that desktop ends; \fBhdw4s pool size\fR with no number shows which\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBSeats are named \fB_hdw4s_0\fR, \fB_hdw4s_1\fR, \|\.\|\.\|\.\fR \-\- fixed, and a name no person's account can have\. Nobody types one: \fBpool size\fR chooses the seats and \fBlist \-\-seats\fR shows them\. Earlier versions called them \fBephemeral0\fR, \fBephemeral1\fR, \|\.\|\.\|\. under \fBHDW4S_EPHEMERAL_PREFIX\fR, which is retired and read by nothing\. A slot table that still offers the old names is refused rather than translated: at boot no seat is minted and \fBhdw4s\-ephemeral\-slots\.service\fR fails naming the rows, and \fBhdw4s check\fR fails the same way\. \fBhdw4s pool size N\fR, with the size the machine already has, replaces them; then \fBsystemctl start hdw4s\-ephemeral\-slots\.service\fR\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBSettings belong to the pool, not to a seat\.\fR \fBhdw4s pool set\fR writes the one file every ephemeral desktop reads; \fBset\fR with a seat's name is refused\. A seat may still carry a setting of its own from an install configured seat by seat, and it still wins for that seat: \fBpool show\fR lists each one, and removing the line from \fB/etc/hdw4s/<seat>\.conf\fR hands the seat back to the pool\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBThe credential belongs to the pool, not to a slot\.\fR A reverse proxy presents one credential to reach the pool; individual slots have none and cannot be given one, which is why \fBauth\fR refuses for a slot\.
.IP "" 0
.SS "The pool's credential"
The pool reads one credential from \fB/etc/hdw4s/demux\.auth\.cred\fR\. Nothing creates that file for you: it is a site's own secret, shared with the reverse proxy, so the package has no business inventing one\. \fBWithout it the pool refuses to start\fR, and says so in the journal rather than serving without authentication\.
.P
It holds one line, \fBuser:password\fR, with no trailing newline required, and should be \fB0600\fR and owned by \fBroot\fR\. Both halves are yours to choose; the proxy has to present the same pair\.
.IP "" 4
.nf
( umask 0077
  printf 'front:%s' "$(head \-c48 /dev/urandom | base64 | tr \-d '\en=/+' | head \-c32)" \e
    > /etc/hdw4s/demux\.auth\.cred )
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Nothing reads the user name except the comparison, so it may be anything that does not contain a colon; the colon is the separator\.
.P
\fBRotating it takes three steps and the third is the one people forget\.\fR Write the new value here, put the matching pair in the reverse proxy, and then restart the pool\. It reads the credential once, at startup, so a file changed underneath a running pool changes nothing until then, and the proxy will be refused with \fB401\fR in the meantime\.
.P
\fBhdw4s pool proxy\fR prints the reverse\-proxy configuration for the pool\.
.P
The pool is served by a unit named \fBhdw4s\-demux\fR, which is where it appears in \fBsystemctl\fR output and in the journal\. Nothing else requires that name\.
.SH "THE SHARED DROP AREA"
Off unless the administrator turns it on\. With it on, every desktop on the machine, named and ephemeral, sees \fB/shared\fR: a directory each of them may read and write, for handing a file from one desktop to another\. The machine's administrator sees the same table at \fB/shared\fR too (see \fBFor the administrator\fR below)\.
.P
\fBOn one machine, this is the whole of the setup:\fR
.IP "" 4
.nf
HDW4S_SHARED=tmpfs
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
in \fB/etc/hdw4s/hdw4s\.conf\fR, and a reboot\. The table is then memory of its own, \fBHDW4S_SHARED_SIZE\fR big (10% of this machine's memory unless set), emptied by every reboot\. Nothing else is needed, and nothing below about stores, sweepers or containers applies\.
.P
It is a picnic table, not a file server, and turning it on deliberately weakens what desktops can do to each other\. Anybody may leave, take, replace or alter anything there, and nothing about an item is promised, not even that it will still be there\. Tell users so\. A file taken from the table is as untrusted as anything else on it \-\- that holds for the administrator's own copies too\.
.P
What is kept is that the table cannot be turned against the rest of the machine\. It is a filesystem of its own, so no hard link can reach it from a home, and it is mounted \fBnosymfollow\fR, \fBnoexec\fR, \fBnosuid\fR and \fBnodev\fR: the kernel follows no link there and nothing there runs by being opened\. Only regular files and directories are kept; anything else is removed\. A program that resolves links itself, instead of asking the kernel to, could follow one in the moment before the sweep removes it\. A program can still be given a file from there to read, as a person can be given a document; that is not something a mount option stops\.
.SS "Settings"
All are read at boot, by \fBhdw4s\-ephemeral\-slots\.service\fR: reboot after changing one\.
.TP
\fBHDW4S_SHARED\fR
\fBoff\fR (the default), \fBtmpfs\fR or \fBsource\fR\. Off means nothing is mounted, no desktop sees \fB/shared\fR, and no file of the feature exists\.
.TP
\fBHDW4S_SHARED_SIZE\fR
\fBtmpfs\fR only: an absolute size (\fB512M\fR, \fB2G\fR) or a percentage of this machine's memory (\fB10%\fR, the default)\. The percentage is worked out from \fB/proc/meminfo\fR: handed to the kernel, it would be taken of the host's memory in a container\. The number of files is capped in proportion (one per 16 KiB), because a size alone does not bound what empty files cost\. What is stored counts against the memory of whoever wrote it\.
.IP
This setting used to switch the feature on\. Set without \fBHDW4S_SHARED\fR, it now does nothing, and the boot and \fBhdw4s check\fR both say so\.
.TP
\fBHDW4S_SHARED_SOURCE\fR
\fBsource\fR only: the root of a store an administrator provides, already mounted\. See \fBThe store\fR below\.
.TP
\fBHDW4S_SHARED_SWEEP\fR
\fBlocal\fR (the default) sweeps the table on this machine\. \fBexternal\fR is for a store shared between machines, where exactly one machine \-\- the one holding the storage \-\- sweeps it, and every other runs no sweep\.
.TP
\fBHDW4S_SHARED_IDLE\fR
An item goes once nobody has read, written or changed it for this long (default \fB30m\fR; the syntax of \fBHDW4S_IDLE_DAYS\fR, a bare number is days)\. An empty directory goes once nothing has been added to it or removed from it for this long; merely listing it does not count, or browsing would keep every empty directory for ever\. A time in the future, from a clock that is wrong or a file stamped by hand, is not believed\.
.TP
\fBHDW4S_SHARED_MAX_AGE\fR
And whatever happens, an item goes once it is this old (default \fB7d\fR), counted from when it was created\. \fB0\fR is refused: what is left there is removed in the end\. On a filesystem that does not record when a file was created this is not enforced, and the sweep and \fBhdw4s check\fR say so\.
.P
\fBHDW4S_SHARED_EXPIRY\fR, from earlier versions, is retired; \fBHDW4S_SHARED_IDLE\fR replaces it, and a leftover line is named by \fBhdw4s check\fR\.
.SS "What runs"
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBThe sweep\fR, every two minutes (\fBhdw4s\-shared\-sweep\fR; see hdw4s\-shared\-sweep(8))\. It makes every item readable and writable by everyone \-\- desktops create files private to their owner, and a copy keeps its mode \-\- and removes what has expired\. Where the kernel allows it, a watcher widens new items as they appear, so the two\-minute wait is the exception\. With \fBHDW4S_SHARED_SWEEP=local\fR, the expose service below keeps both running while the table is shown: one stopped by an upgrade or by hand is started again within 30 seconds\. \fBhdw4s check\fR is red while the sweep is not running \-\- nothing would ever expire \-\- and warns while the watcher is not\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fB\fBhdw4s\-shared\-expose\.service\fR\fR, every 30 seconds\. Desktops bind \fB/run/hdw4s/shared\fR as \fB/shared\fR; this binds the store's \fBtable\fR onto that directory once the store passes the guard, and takes it away again when the store stops answering, is not mounted, or has lost a mount flag\. A desktop that is already running sees the table arrive and leave\. It only ever unmounts a mount it made itself and recorded: anything else found mounted at \fB/run/hdw4s/shared\fR is left where it is, named in its log, and \fBhdw4s check\fR is red until somebody removes it\.
.IP "" 0
.P
\fBIt fails soft\.\fR While there is no acceptable store, desktops start as usual and see an empty \fB/shared\fR they cannot write to\. A desktop started in the few seconds after a network store stops answering, before the next run notices, may still fail to start; that run then takes the store away\.
.P
\fBThat needs a store that fails rather than hangs\.\fR Mount a network store \fBsoft\fR; NFS mounts are \fBhard\fR unless told otherwise\. A store that stops answering without failing \-\- a \fBhard\fR NFS mount, or a FUSE filesystem whose daemon has stopped \-\- also stalls the run that would take it away, so \fB/shared\fR stays bound to it, and every desktop started until the store answers again hangs at start\. \fBhdw4s check\fR warns about an NFS store mounted \fBhard\fR\.
.P
\fB/run/hdw4s/shared\fR itself is made at every boot, empty and root's, whether the feature is on or not\. \fBDo not delete it\fR: every desktop with \fB/shared\fR then fails to start (226/NAMESPACE), on purpose \-\- the alternative systemd offers, an optional bind, would leave a \fB/shared\fR on the root filesystem writable by every desktop\. \fBsystemd\-tmpfiles \-\-create\fR makes it again, but root's \fB/shared\fR keeps showing the deleted one until the next boot, and \fBhdw4s check\fR is red until then\.
.P
\fBFor the administrator:\fR in the root namespace \fB/shared\fR is a read\-only view that shows the table only while it is guarded: the boot makes it, as a read\-only bind of \fB/run/hdw4s/shared\fR that receives whatever is bound there and nothing else, so the table appears and leaves with the expose service's bind\. While no table is shown it is empty and refuses writes; while one is, it is the table, writable as the desktops' is\. Copy out with a plain \fBcp\fR; there is nothing on the table but regular files and directories\. Expiry applies to what the administrator leaves there, too\. \fBGive the tools the store path, never /shared\fR \-\- \fBhdw4s\-shared\-sweep \-\-dir\fR takes the store's own mount or \fB/run/hdw4s/shared\fR\.
.P
That view is made only while no table is shown yet: made later, it would keep the table after the expose service takes it away\. So turning the feature on with an upgrade, or reinstalling while a table is shown, leaves it for the next boot, and says so\. A \fB/shared\fR that was there before the feature made one (an administrator's own) is left alone; desktops see the table there, root does not, and \fBhdw4s check\fR says so\. Turning the feature off removes the view and the directory again, if this feature made them and once no desktop is running: removing it under a running desktop would take \fB/shared\fR away from that desktop\. Do not remove \fB/shared\fR by hand while desktops run, for the same reason\.
.P
\fBNever unmount \fB/shared\fR in the root namespace by hand\.\fR Every desktop, and every service systemd starts in a namespace of its own, holds a copy of that view\. Unmounted while a table is shown, the copy of the table on each is left behind in those namespaces: it no longer goes away when the table is taken away (a lost flag, a dead store, \fBapt remove\fR), and it keeps the store busy\. Nothing on the machine can see it from outside those namespaces\. \fBhdw4s check\fR is red once it notices the view was unmounted\. To repair it, restart every desktop and namespaced service started since the boot, or reboot\. To take the view away on purpose, turn the feature off and reboot\.
.SS "The store"
In \fBtmpfs\fR mode the package makes the store itself, at \fB/run/hdw4s/shared\-store\fR, with a root only root can enter: desktops reach the table through \fB/shared\fR and no other way\. The front door's router, the one process here that strangers on the network talk to, has no use for the table; it is hidden from it at start, but a table bound again later (after an outage, say) becomes visible to it until the router restarts\. In \fBsource\fR mode, \fBHDW4S_SHARED_SOURCE\fR names one, and it must be exactly this, or it is refused and \fB/shared\fR stays empty:
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBA whole filesystem of its own\fR, mounted at \fBHDW4S_SHARED_SOURCE\fR \-\- not a directory inside some other filesystem, which would let a hard link reach whatever else is on it\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBIts root owned by root\fR, not writable by group or others, holding \fBtable/\fR (mode 0777, not sticky: the sticky bit would stop anyone replacing or taking an item) \fBand nothing else\fR, apart from a \fBlost+found\fR or a \fB\.zfs\fR\. Only \fBtable\fR is ever shown to desktops or swept\. Inside an unprivileged container a root owned by the host's root shows as the overflow uid (usually 65534, \fBnobody\fR), and is accepted as root's\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBMounted \fBnosymfollow,nodev,noexec,nosuid\fR and \fBstrictatime\fR\.\fR On NFS, \fBstrictatime\fR is the server's business rather than the client's: the server's filesystem must record every access (for ZFS, \fBatime=on relatime=off\fR), which nothing on the client can verify\. Where a flag is missing the package adds it to that mount in place, once, and logs it \-\- never a second mount beside it\.
.IP "" 0
.P
A store not mounted at all leaves the empty directory underneath, which is not a filesystem's root and has no \fBtable\fR, so it is refused rather than used\.
.SS "Sharing one table between machines"
Mount the store \fBon the host\fR, with the flags, and bind it into each container, carrying the flags on the bind\. If the store is on another machine, the host mounts it over NFS\. In each container set \fBHDW4S_SHARED=source\fR, \fBHDW4S_SHARED_SOURCE\fR to where the bind lands, and \fBHDW4S_SHARED_SWEEP=external\fR; install \fBhdw4s\-shared\-sweep\fR on the machine that holds the storage and run its sweep there, and nowhere else\. The package in a container checks what it is given and does nothing else, unless a flag is missing\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBLXC, and Proxmox VE\fR (in \fB/etc/pve/lxc/<id>\.conf\fR), one line per container, the container path without its leading slash:
.IP "" 4
.nf
lxc\.mount\.entry: <host store> <path in container> none bind,create=dir,optional,nosymfollow,nodev,noexec,nosuid,strictatime 0 0
.fi
.IP "" 0
.IP
\fBThe host mount must already carry all of these flags\fR (\fBnosymfollow\fR, \fBnodev\fR, \fBnoexec\fR, \fBnosuid\fR, \fBstrictatime\fR); the entry can only pass them through\. If any of them differs \-\- a host mount that is \fBrelatime\fR while the entry says \fBstrictatime\fR is enough \-\- the container receives the bind with \fBno flags at all\fR, \fBoptional\fR hides that the remount failed, and hdw4s refuses it: \fB/shared\fR stays empty\. Verify inside the container:
.IP "" 4
.nf
findmnt \-o TARGET,OPTIONS <path in container>
.fi
.IP "" 0
.IP
\fBoptional\fR lets the container boot when the host path is missing; the guard then refuses the empty directory, which is the fail\-soft case\. On Proxmox VE a \fBmpN:\fR mount point also works, but takes part in snapshots, backups and migration, so the entry above is preferred\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBIncus and LXD:\fR a disk device whose source is the host's flagged mount\. Whether it carries the flags through, or behaves like an LXC entry when one differs, has not been measured: verify with \fBfindmnt\fR inside\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBA virtual machine:\fR an NFS mount carrying the flags, for example \fBnosymfollow,nodev,noexec,nosuid,soft\fR\.
.IP "" 0
.P
\fBFor migration or high availability\fR, the host path in the entry must name the same store on every node the container may run on: present the store at one path everywhere \-\- locally where the storage is, over NFS elsewhere\.
.SS "Caveats"
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBIn a container, \fBnosymfollow\fR holds against the container's users, not against its root\.\fR The other three flags arrive locked from the host, but container root can remount without \fBnosymfollow\fR\. The guard notices at its next run and takes the table away; it catches accident and misconfiguration, not a hostile root, who is trusted with the machine\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBReads served from another machine's NFS cache do not count as use\.\fR The server records the first read and not the ones after it, so an item only ever read from a machine that is not the storage host can expire \fBHDW4S_SHARED_IDLE\fR after it was last written or first read\. \fBHDW4S_SHARED_MAX_AGE\fR is unaffected\. The optional usage relay of hdw4s\-shared\-sweep(8) closes this; without it, nothing else changes\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBAn NFS store should be mounted \fBsoft\fR\.\fR A \fBhard\fR mount that stops answering blocks everything that touches it until it answers again, and \fBhdw4s check\fR warns about one\.
.IP "" 0
.SS "hdw4s check"
Red when \fB/shared\fR is on and nothing is bound at \fB/run/hdw4s/shared\fR, when what is bound fails the guard, when \fBHDW4S_SHARED\fR or its expiry settings cannot be read, and when nobody is sweeping: some item has gone unused for longer than \fBHDW4S_SHARED_IDLE\fR plus two sweeps \-\- on a machine with \fBHDW4S_SHARED_SWEEP=external\fR, the only sign it has that the sweeper elsewhere has stopped\. A warning, not a failure, for a store more than 90% full, an NFS store mounted \fBhard\fR, a filesystem that does not record creation times, the old \fBHDW4S_SHARED_SIZE\fR switch, and for a day after the table was taken away for a lost flag and bound again ("drift healed at \|\.\|\.\|\."): a repair is never silent\. In \fBtmpfs\fR mode it also says how big the table is, default or not\.
.P
Red, too, when \fB/shared\fR in the root namespace is anything but the read\-only view of \fB/run/hdw4s/shared\fR described above \-\- a view that kept the table after it was taken away, one whose source was deleted, one that is writable, or anything else mounted there \-\- and when the feature is on and the boot left no view at all\. A view left for the next boot is a warning; an administrator's own \fB/shared\fR is named, not counted\.
.SH "TRANSPORTS"
How the reverse proxy reaches a session, in decreasing order of how much the machine itself can guarantee\.
.TP
\fBUnix socket\fR (\fBhdw4s transport\fR \fIinstance\fR \fBunix\fR)
The session listens only on loopback, and systemd exposes it at \fB/run/hdw4s/proxy/<instance>\.sock\fR, owned by the group named in \fBHDW4S_PROXY_GROUP\fR\. Who may connect is then a question of file permissions: a process that cannot open the socket cannot reach the session at all, whatever address it comes from and whatever it claims to be\. There is no shared secret to leak and no address to spoof\.
.IP
This is the right answer whenever it is available\. It requires the proxy to be on the same machine, or to be able to see the same filesystem\. For an ephemeral slot it is the only answer: a visitor reaches one well\-known hostname and the pool on this machine chooses the slot, so there is no caller outside the machine for a port to serve \-\- and a port would be open to every account in the container, which is not a caller the session has\.
.IP
Two containers on one host can share a socket if the same directory is bind\-mounted into both and they use the same user\-id mapping, which is the usual default\. It then behaves exactly as it would locally: file permissions decide who may connect, and \fBSO_PEERCRED\fR reports the peer's identity translated correctly into the reading side's namespace\. Containers with \fIdifferent\fR id mappings see different ownership on the same file, and that is where the arrangement stops working\.
.TP
\fBTCP with a proxy credential\fR (\fBtcp\fR plus \fBhdw4s auth\fR)
For a proxy on another machine\. The firewall restricts which addresses may connect, and the session additionally requires a secret that only the proxy knows, injected by it as a header\. An attacker who defeats the address check \-\- by spoofing, or simply by being on the same network \-\- still does not have the secret\.
.IP
The secret crosses the network in the clear on each request, so it is worth pairing with something that makes the path itself private, such as a point\-to\-point encrypted tunnel between the proxy and the session's host\.
.IP
It is also worth making the address check mean something\. Where the network can pin each host to the addresses it was assigned, a neighbour can no longer claim to be the proxy, which is otherwise the easy way past an address list\. Most hypervisors and many switches offer some form of this; consult their documentation, and check afterwards that a dynamically assigned address has not been left out\.
.TP
\fBTCP alone\fR (\fBtcp\fR, the default)
The firewall's address list is the only control\. Adequate on a network where nothing untrusted can route to the machine, and the weakest of the three otherwise\.
.SH "REVERSE PROXY AND SECURITY"
\fBA session performs no authentication of its own\.\fR It is built to sit behind a reverse proxy that has already identified the user, so that reaching the desktop takes no second login\. Anything that can open a TCP connection to a session port gets that user's desktop\.
.P
Two things follow, and both matter:
.IP "\[ci]" 4
\fBHDW4S_PROXIES\fR must list the proxy, and nothing else\. It is the only thing standing between a session and everything that can route to the machine\.
.IP "\[ci]" 4
The proxy must be the only path\. Terminate TLS there, authenticate there, and do not expose a session port by any other route\.
.IP "" 0
.P
Two things sharpen the second point\.
.P
The newest connection wins\. A client that opens a desktop somebody else is using takes it, and the identity a client presents is its own claim rather than something the session checks\. So where an unauthenticated reach at a session port once meant a connection that was refused, it now means one that can take a running desktop away from the person at it\.
.P
And the streaming server offers more than a desktop unless it is told not to\. Its own defaults serve a file manager over the user's \fBDesktop\fR directory, accept uploads into it, and admit extra viewers and gamepad players to a live session\. hdw4s turns each of those off at the server, by name rather than by relying on a default, because they share a URL prefix with the stream and a proxy cannot separate them\. Two endpoints, \fB/api/status\fR and \fB/api/health\fR, answer before any authentication runs and always will\.
.P
The remedy for all of it is the same as it has always been, and it is why \fBHDW4S_PROXIES\fR exists; this only raises what is lost by getting it wrong\.
.P
The package owns one nftables table, \fBinet hdw4s\fR\. It restricts and does not grant\. A drop in this table overrides any other table's accept, so the port block can be closed reliably\. An accept in it is only advisory: if another firewall on the machine drops the packet first, nothing here brings it back, so opening the block to the proxy may need a rule wherever that firewall is configured\.
.P
The rules cover the whole port block rather than the ports currently in use\. A rule written per session goes stale the moment a session moves, which is how a firewall ends up guarding a port nothing listens on while live sessions sit unprotected beside it\.
.P
The table also stops one desktop reaching another\. A desktop is meant to be reached through its own front door, which is where authentication happens; before this rule existed, anything running inside one session could open a connection straight to another session's port over loopback, with no credential \-\- past the reverse proxy for a named desktop, and past every boundary there is for an ephemeral one, whose occupant is a stranger running untrusted code\. The rule matches the \fIsender\fR: a connection to a session port is refused when it comes from a process inside \fBhdw4s\.slice\fR, which is every desktop and nothing else\. The relay that serves the sessions runs under \fBsystem\.slice\fR and is unaffected, as is anything an administrator runs\. Nothing per session is recorded, so there is nothing here to go stale when a slot moves\.
.P
Two exceptions, both deliberate\. Connections made as \fBroot\fR from inside a session are allowed\. This was added for the session's own startup check of what its server was serving, which was made that way; that check now reads the streaming server's own startup announcement out of the journal and opens no connection at all, so the exception has no caller in what this package ships and is kept only because removing a firewall rule deserves its own measurement\. It concedes little either way: the occupant of an ephemeral slot is in no sudo\-capable group and cannot become root, and on a named desktop anyone who could would be able to flush the table outright\. And the rule needs a kernel that can match a socket by its cgroup \-\- 6\.11 or newer inside a container\. Where that is not available, \fBhdw4s firewall \-\-apply\fR says so on standard error and \fBhdw4s firewall \-\-check\fR reports \fBcross\-reach NOT CLOSED\fR\. There is no port\-range fallback for this one: the relay talks to the same ports the desktops do, so a rule written by port would either close the product or close nothing\.
.P
A desktop that needs to reach a service elsewhere goes out over the network and authenticates like any other client\.
.P
The table is also checked periodically, not just applied once at session start\. \fBnft flush ruleset\fR removes every table on the machine, including this one, and several common tools issue it \-\- \fBufw reload\fR and \fBnetfilter\-persistent\fR among them\. Without a check, the table would vanish while every session carried on serving, and nothing would say so\. \fBhdw4s\-firewall\.timer\fR notices and puts it back\.
.P
\fBhdw4s enable\fR refuses an account below UID 1000 unless \fBHDW4S_ALLOW_SYSTEM_USER=yes\fR is set, since a browser\-reachable desktop with no authentication of its own is rarely what is wanted for a system account\.
.P
Each session's X server is given its own authority cookie in its runtime directory\. Without one, every account on the machine could read the session's screen and type into it\.
.P
A session's streaming server never listens on a port\. Every desktop on a machine shares one network namespace, so a port is a name any of them could take first and be connected to in place of the desktop it belongs to\. It listens at \fB/run/hdw4s/stream/<instance>/s/stream\.sock\fR instead, in a directory made afresh for each start that only the session's own account may create in and only it and the relay's group, \fBhdw4s\-relay\fR, may enter\. An account in that group could therefore open every desktop on the machine, so a session refuses to start as one\.
.SH "FILES"
.TP
\fB/etc/hdw4s/hdw4s\.conf\fR
Defaults for every session\.
.TP
\fB/etc/hdw4s/<instance>\.conf\fR
Settings for one session\.
.TP
\fB/etc/hdw4s/instances\fR
Slot table mapping instances to ports\. Edit with \fBhdw4s release\fR, not by hand while a session is running\.
.TP
\fB/etc/hdw4s/<instance>\.auth\.cred\fR
The credential the reverse proxy has to present, encrypted to this machine\. Created by \fBhdw4s auth\fR and by \fBhdw4s enable\fR for a session it creates; removed by \fBhdw4s noauth\fR and by \fBhdw4s release\fR\.
.TP
\fB/etc/hdw4s/<instance>\.keyring\.cred\fR
The session keyring's password, encrypted to this machine\. Created by \fBhdw4s keyring\fR, removed by \fBhdw4s release\fR\.
.TP
\fB/etc/hdw4s/nftables\.conf\fR
Generated ruleset\. Regenerated by \fBhdw4s firewall \-\-apply\fR\.
.TP
\fB/etc/sysctl\.d/60\-hdw4s\.conf\fR
Reserves the session port block so that the kernel never hands one of those ports to an outgoing connection from something else, which would otherwise stop a session binding, intermittently\. Written by \fBhdw4s firewall \-\-apply\fR\.
.TP
\fB/etc/systemd/system/hdw4s@<instance>\.service\.d/\fR
Drop\-ins written by \fBhdw4s enable\fR: the account the session runs as, the home directory it may write to and is ordered after, a profile directory outside the default, and the credentials to load\. Written by the tool, not by hand \-\- \fBhdw4s enable\fR is safe to re\-run and rewrites them\.
.TP
\fB/etc/systemd/system/hdw4s\-proxy@<instance>\.socket\.d/\fR
The address the front door listens on, written by \fBhdw4s transport\fR\.
.TP
\fB/run/hdw4s/\fR
Everything hdw4s keeps while the machine is up: each desktop's runtime directory under \fBsession/\fR, and beside it the sockets, stream directories, requests and records listed here\. A reboot empties it\. After installing a version that changed where these live, reboot: a desktop still running keeps using the old places until it is restarted\.
.TP
\fB/run/hdw4s/shared/\fR
What every desktop binds as \fB/shared\fR: made empty at every boot, with the table bound onto it while \fB/shared\fR is on and a store passes\. See \fBTHE SHARED DROP AREA\fR\.
.TP
\fB/run/hdw4s/ledger/<instance>\fR
A named desktop's failed runs inside the last twenty minutes, one line each, written by root when a run ends\. Three refuse the next start; see \fBINSTANCES\fR\. Removing it clears the refusal\.
.TP
\fB/run/hdw4s/stream/<instance>/s/\fR
Where the session's streaming server listens\. Made by \fBhdw4s\-stream@<instance>\.service\fR when the session starts, removed when it stops\.
.TP
\fB/usr/lib/hdw4s/hdw4s\.xorg\.conf\fR
X server configuration for the \fBdummy\fR driver\. Deliberately not \fB/etc/X11/xorg\.conf\fR, which belongs to the machine's own display\.
.TP
\fB/var/lib/hdw4s/<instance>/\fR
An isolated session's profile\.
.TP
\fB/run/hdw4s/session/<instance>/\fR
Runtime directory: X authority cookie, X server log, current display\.
.TP
\fB/run/hdw4s/proxy/<instance>\.sock\fR
The filesystem socket a session is reached through, where \fBhdw4s transport\fR has been told to use one\.
.SH "UPDATES"
A timer follows upstream daily, installing the distribution package upstream publishes\. What it downloads is checked against a sha256 \-\- the one recorded in \fBhdw4s\-update\fR for the release and platform the maintainer tested, otherwise the digest GitHub publishes for that asset \-\- and the download is refused if neither is available, so nothing is ever installed unchecked\. It also reads the package's own control fields back and checks its declared dependencies against the machine before installing, rather than unpacking something that then cannot be configured\. That pins the bytes to what somebody saw; it is not a signature, and upstream publishes none\. See SECURITY\.md\.
.P
It restarts only sessions nobody is connected to\. A session in use keeps the version it started with until it next stops on its own, which is when picking up a new one costs nothing\. An updater that reboots a desktop somebody is working in, at an hour chosen by a timer, is an updater that gets switched off\.
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS"
.nf
hdw4s list                       what exists and whether it is running
hdw4s check                      whether a desktop can be handed out at all
hdw4s show <instance>            effective settings and their source
hdw4s firewall \-\-check           whether the table is loaded and what it covers
hdw4s \-\-version                  which version this is
systemctl status hdw4s@<i>       includes the display and port when running
journalctl \-xeu hdw4s@<i>        session output, including the X server
journalctl \-\-namespace=hdw4s\-<slot>
                                 an ephemeral desktop's own output
.fi
.P
An ephemeral desktop logs into a journal of its own, not the machine's: kept in memory only, readable by root only, and emptied when the visit ends\. Starts and stops still appear in the machine's journal (\fBjournalctl \-u hdw4s\-ephemeral@<slot>\fR); what happened inside the desktop does not, and is gone once the visitor has left\. Look while the desktop is running\. On a machine with a persistent journal the machine's own journal would otherwise give each slot's reused uid a file of its own, so the next visitor could read the previous one's output\.
.P
Printing from an ephemeral desktop goes only to the visitor's own browser, as a PDF through the streaming server's print queue; the machine's printers are neither listed nor reachable from it\. Named desktops keep the machine's printers\.
.P
\fBhdw4s check\fR asks three questions, and the first two are about the machine rather than about any session\.
.P
The first is whether the memory limit on the desktop slice is a number this machine can reach\. A soft limit on \fBhdw4s\.slice\fR is what makes one session that overreaches go slow instead of making every other session go slow, and a limit set at or above the machine's own total memory is never reached by anything \-\- so it is not a limit, and nothing on the machine shows that\. The usual cause is a percentage: \fBsystemd expands a percentage in a memory limit through \fBsysconf(_SC_PHYS_PAGES)\fR, which a container cannot virtualise, while \fB/proc/meminfo\fR, which it can, stays correct\.\fR Inside a 16 GiB container that made \fBMemoryHigh=75%\fR mean 46\.58 GiB \-\- three times the container's memory, never applied once\. \fBfree\fR, \fBtop\fR and \fB/proc/meminfo\fR all reported 16 GiB, correctly, throughout; the only place the disagreement was visible was in what systemd had resolved the directive to\. This product therefore computes the value from \fB/proc/meminfo\fR and writes it as an absolute byte count at boot, and this check is what notices if anything puts a percentage back\. A limit \fIbelow\fR what the machine has is not reported: it is either right or merely conservative\.
.P
The second is whether the machine can hand anybody a desktop\. On a box with ephemeral slots configured it reports a slot table that exists and cannot be read, slot identities that were never minted, a front door with nothing bound to its port, a slot whose socket has no listener, and any unit of the pool's that has latched into \fBfailed\fR\. Each of those is a state in which an arrival is refused while every session on the machine looks fine, because there are none: an ephemeral pool starts a desktop when somebody knocks, so ZERO RUNNING SESSIONS IS THE NORMAL STATE OF A HEALTHY IDLE MACHINE, and it is also what a pool that can start nothing looks like\. The count alone cannot tell them apart, which is why the summary line says how many slot doors are listening as well as how many sessions are running\.
.P
One state it does not see, and the omission is deliberate rather than pending: a slot the router has already let to a visitor who never arrived is listening, unfailed and unserviceable\. Whether a slot is let is the router's own record, not systemd's, so the answer has to come from the router\.
.P
It also reports any named desktop that has failed or is refusing to start (see \fBINSTANCES\fR), whether or not anything else is wrong: such a desktop refuses its owner on every visit while every running session looks fine\.
.P
The third question is asked of every session that is running now: is it serving the incarnation token its own start published? A returning browser tab compares its saved token against the served one to decide whether it is looking at the desktop it had\. A session serving nothing makes that comparison empty on both sides, which reads as "unchanged" \-\- so the tab resumes into whichever session now holds the slot, instead of asking\.
.P
That state is not caused by a broken session\. It is caused by a session that was already running when something below it changed: a slot started before the publisher unit first ran, and never restarted, goes on running without it and is reported \fBactive\fR by everything that looks\. A restart fixes one; the check is what notices the next one\.
.P
The same pass reports any running session that started without the WebRTC it is configured to offer\. With \fBHDW4S_WEBRTC=yes\fR, the default, a session starts through an adapter that keeps the streaming server's WebRTC signalling off TCP\. When an update moves the code that adapter changes, it does not refuse to start the desktop, and it does not let WebRTC run unadapted: the desktop starts on the WebSocket alone with the transport switch locked off, exactly as with \fBHDW4S_WEBRTC=no\fR, and says why in the session's journal and here\. Update hdw4s, or set \fBHDW4S_WEBRTC=no\fR for the session, to clear it\.
.P
It is run every fifteen minutes by \fBhdw4s\-check\.timer\fR, which marks \fBhdw4s\-check\.service\fR failed when it finds anything, so \fBsystemctl \-\-failed\fR names it\. Run as root: the record it compares against is readable by root only, and asked by anyone else the command reports that it could not tell rather than that nothing was wrong\.
.P
The X server's log is kept in the session's runtime directory rather than the home directory, so it disappears with the session instead of accumulating\.
.SH "LIMITATIONS"
There is no way to configure STUN\. A session starts on a single WebSocket and opens no UDP socket until the person in it switches to WebRTC from the side menu, which \fBHDW4S_WEBRTC=yes\fR (the default) offers\. From then on the browser must reach the machine directly; with \fBHDW4S_MEDIA_PORTS=proxied\fR the firewall admits that only from the proxy addresses, so elsewhere the switch fails to connect\. The server runs ICE\-lite and contacts no STUN server itself\.
.P
TURN is a relay of your own or none: \fBHDW4S_TURN_HOST\fR and \fBHDW4S_TURN_SECRET\fR name one, and without them a session is pinned to a host that cannot resolve\. The secret is readable by every account with a desktop on that machine, because the streaming server runs as that account and has to hold it to sign each browser's credential\.
.P
A viewer joined to a shared session can switch its transport, restarting the stream for everyone\. The streaming server does not yet confine the switch to the session's own user; \fBHDW4S_WEBRTC=no\fR locks it off\.
.P
Under 1\.6 this was not true, and the reasons it mattered are worth keeping: the server appended its own default STUN server unless one was named exactly, so a setting could only ever add a second and never replace the first, and every session told a third party the machine's address and when it started\. TURN was disabled outright because the server otherwise used a relay belonging to a third party with a shared secret published in its own source, which would have carried the desktop's video, keystrokes and clipboard\.
.P
An account gets one desktop per machine\. The same account having desktops on \fIdifferent\fR machines, against one home directory, is a supported case and is what \fBHDW4S_ISOLATION=profile\fR exists for\.
.P
\fBsendmail\fR does not work inside a session; SMTP to \fBlocalhost\fR does\. The two differ because the local \fBsendmail\fR command does not talk to the mail system over the network \-\- it writes into the queue directory through \fBpostdrop\fR \-\- and \fBProtectSystem=strict\fR makes that directory read\-only\. It fails with \fBRead\-only file system\fR and the message is lost, leaving only a warning in the journal\.
.P
The mail system itself is outside the session and perfectly reachable\. A session shares the machine's network namespace, so submitting to \fB127\.0\.0\.1:25\fR works and is the supported route\. Measured from inside a running session, as the session's own account: the banner answers, the message is accepted, and the local mail system relays it onward \-\- \fBstatus=sent\fR, a real delivery to the smarthost\.
.P
So a mail client needs nothing: they all speak SMTP already\. The gap affects programs that shell out to \fBsendmail\fR, \fBmail\fR(1) among them\. To close it, install a \fBsendmail\fR that submits over SMTP rather than through the queue directory, and let it take the name:
.IP "" 4
.nf
apt\-get install msmtp

cat > /etc/msmtprc <<'END'
defaults
auth       off
tls        off
syslog     on

account    local
host       127\.0\.0\.1
port       25

account default : local
END

dpkg\-divert \-\-divert /usr/sbin/sendmail\.orig \-\-rename \-\-add /usr/sbin/sendmail
ln \-sfn /usr/bin/msmtp /usr/sbin/sendmail
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Install \fBmsmtp\fR, not \fBmsmtp\-mta\fR\. The latter claims to \fIbe\fR the machine's mail system, and asking for it proposes removing the one already there \-\- \fBapt\-get install \-s msmtp\-mta\fR answers \fBRemv postfix\fR, taking \fBbsd\-mailx\fR with it\. That mail system is still wanted: it keeps the queue, the retries and the onward relay, and none of this changes any of them, only how a message is handed over\. The diversion is what survives an upgrade of the mail system's own package; \fBdpkg\-divert \-\-rename \-\-remove /usr/sbin/sendmail\fR puts it back\.
.P
One property is given up\. \fBmsmtp\fR needs the mail system listening at the moment of the call and reports an error when it is not, where the queue directory would have held the message for collection later\. On a machine whose mail system is a running service that is a small trade, and a visible error is an improvement on what it replaces, which was a silent loss\.
.P
This package is for machines with no screen\. A session for an account that is also logged in at a physical console on the same machine is not supported and not tested\. It is not prevented either, and no reason is known why it could not work \-\- the conflict it would cause is the one \fBHDW4S_ISOLATION=profile\fR addresses \-\- but nothing here detects it or warns about it, and two desktops writing one account's settings corrupt them rather than reporting anything\.
.P
A session gives GNOME a private D\-Bus, so gnome\-session cannot reach a systemd user manager and falls back to its own service manager\. Two things follow, and both are load\-bearing rather than incidental\. The desktop's components stay inside the session's control group, which is what lets \fBKillMode=control\-group\fR reap the whole desktop when the unit stops \-\- as user units they would belong to the account's \fBuser@\.service\fR and survive it\. And the package depends on that fallback existing at all: \fBGNOME 49 removes it and GNOME 50 replaces it with a hard refusal, and GNOME 50 also removes the X11 session\fR the current Selkies release needs\. Neither version works here, and the limit is on the package rather than on any optional part of it\.
.P
That limit does not reach the rest of the design\. The X server, the streaming, the audio and the isolation are all indifferent to which desktop is started, so setting \fBHDW4S_SESSION\fR to one that is not being withdrawn from X11 \-\- Xfce, MATE, LXQt \-\- avoids both halves of it\. That is a smaller change than it sounds, and a much smaller one than bridging a Wayland session back onto an X11 display, which is possible but visibly a workaround\.
.P
Snap and Flatpak applications ignore the wrappers and some of the environment used to isolate a session, so they may still share state between desktops\.
.P
A session runs with \fBCAP_NET_RAW\fR in its capability bounding set and nothing else, which is what lets \fBping(8)\fR work\. Nothing is granted by that: a program still needs the capability on its own file, and marking a file needs \fBCAP_SETFCAP\fR, which the session does not have\. Tools that capture packets are a different matter and do not work in a session at all, whatever capabilities they carry, because \fBAF_PACKET\fR is not among the address families the unit permits\. Run those over \fBssh(1)\fR instead, where none of these restrictions apply\. An empty bounding set was tried first and is a trap worth naming: the distribution ships \fBping\fR with the effective bit set, the kernel refuses to execute a binary whose effective capabilities cannot be granted, and the result is \fBOperation not permitted\fR from \fBexec\fR with nothing said about sockets or capabilities\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
\fB0\fR
The command did what it was asked to\.
.TP
\fB1\fR
It failed, and said why on standard error\. \fBhdw4s firewall \-\-check\fR also exits 1 when the check itself was fine but what it found was not \-\- a missing table, a session outside the loaded port range, a \fBproxies\fR set that no longer matches the configuration\. That is a result, not a fault, which is why the check is worth running from a timer\.
.TP
\fB2\fR
The command line was wrong: an unknown command, or the wrong number of arguments\. Nothing was changed\.
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.TP
\fBHDW4S_ETCDIR\fR
The configuration directory, \fB/etc/hdw4s\fR by default\. It exists so that the test suite can run against a disposable copy; changing it on a real machine points the tool at one set of files while the units and the firewall go on reading another\.
.TP
\fBPYTHON\fR
The interpreter \fBhdw4s firewall \-\-check\fR uses to read nftables' JSON output\. Defaults to \fBpython3\fR\.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
\fBsystemd\.unit\fR(5), \fBsystemd\.exec\fR(5), \fBnft\fR(8), \fBXorg\fR(1), \fBXserver\fR(1), \fBxauth\fR(1), \fBdbus\-run\-session\fR(1), \fBdconf\fR(7)
